## Timer
//@ [] 00:40

```plotly
function(state) {
    const seconds = 20;
    const max = seconds * 1000;
    const now = new Date();
    if (!state.start) {
        state.start = now;
    }
    let elapsed = now - state.start;
    if (elapsed > max) {
        elapsed = max;
    }
    return {
      data: [
              {
                y: [''],
                x: [elapsed],
                type: 'bar',
                orientation: 'h',
                text: [Math.round((max-elapsed) / 1000)],
                textposition: 'inside',
                textfont: {
                  color: 'white',
                  size: 36
                }
              },
              {
                y: [''],
                x: [max-elapsed],
                type: 'bar',
                orientation: 'h'
              }
            ],
      layout: {
        width:1500,
        height:250,
        barmode: 'stack',
        showlegend: false,
        xaxis: {
          visible: false
        }
      },
      repeat: 200,
      state: state
   };
}
```

/*
Using state, current date and refreshs, we can simulate a timer.
The timer duration (here 20 seconds) is hardcoded.
The state is just the starting date which is set only once at first call.
At each refresh, we compute the elapsed time between the starting date and the current date and we draw a bar plot accordingly.
*/
